{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>For stolen I was stolen.</b> The reason for the doubling of <b>גֻנֹּב</b> and so forth, is to say that two acts of theft were perpetrated upon him: the theft of his body and the theft of the buyers' mind, for they thought he was a slave, by which means they purchased him from them. Therefore he said the word for theft twice. And from now on, behold, he belongs to himself and his body is not acquired by the purchaser. Or perhaps Joseph intended to refute the custom of Egypt, that no slave may rule nor dress [in royal garments] and so forth (Genesis Rabbah 89:6), and since he saw in his dream that he would rise to greatness, he sought to remove the erroneous impression that he was a slave. And it is possible that HaShem did not take him to task for this matter, for a person must remove a stumbling block [from himself], and HaShem took him to task only for his saying ״זְכַרְתַּנִי״ and so forth." "And his saying <b>וגם פה</b> means, in this city, <b>I did nothing</b>. And this is as a proof that they proved [his innocence] from the garment, as they of blessed memory said (Targum Yonatan, Genesis 39:14), for they examined the garment of the wicked woman and found falsehood on its right side. And this Joseph repaid to the priests of Pharaoh who had examined the garment and brought forth against him an evil report; therefore he gave them their portion in full — the allotment that Pharaoh had assigned them (Genesis 47:22) — for without this he would have harbored grievances against them, since ״וְעַל פִּיךָ יִשַּׁק כָּל עַמִּי״ (Genesis 41:40) Pharaoh said to him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {0 "The citation (בראשית רבה פט,ז) normalized to Genesis Rabbah 89:6."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:21.021865897Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}